1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:03,663
Gold that cost 1.01 million won a don in

2
00:00:03,663 --> 00:00:05,457
January now fetches 640,000.

3
00:00:05,457 --> 00:00:08,340
Should you sell the baby ring in your drawer?

4
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:11,586
Short answer: there's one test that matters

5
00:00:11,586 --> 00:00:13,520
more than guessing the gold price.

6
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:16,770
I'll show you that test in the next eight minutes.

7
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:19,007
First, the numbers.

8
00:00:19,007 --> 00:00:21,504
A jeweler pays 640,000 won a don,

9
00:00:21,504 --> 00:00:22,790
as of October 26.

10
00:00:22,990 --> 00:00:24,816
On the Korea Exchange gold market,

11
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:27,179
where you trade gold in a brokerage account,

12
00:00:27,179 --> 00:00:30,670
it peaked at 1.01 million on January 29 and is about 640,000 now.

13
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:33,122
Down 37% in nine months.

14
00:00:33,122 --> 00:00:36,700
As if one in every three gold rings simply vanished.

15
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:41,009
And international gold slipped below 4,000 dollars an ounce,

16
00:00:41,009 --> 00:00:42,530
about 31 grams, last week.

17
00:00:43,330 --> 00:00:44,956
But one number is odd.

18
00:00:44,956 --> 00:00:49,316
International gold is down about 29% from its January high,

19
00:00:49,316 --> 00:00:52,050
yet Korean exchange gold is down 37%.

20
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:53,850
Why did Korea fall further?

21
00:00:55,550 --> 00:00:57,580
Let's rewind to this January.

22
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:00,738
On the Korea Exchange gold market,

23
00:01:00,738 --> 00:01:03,846
one don went from 783,000 won on January 2 to

24
00:01:03,846 --> 00:01:07,230
1.01 million on January 29: up 29% in four weeks.

25
00:01:08,030 --> 00:01:10,396
On January 26 international gold topped 5,000

26
00:01:10,396 --> 00:01:12,289
dollars an ounce for the first time,

27
00:01:12,289 --> 00:01:14,340
and by late January it was above 5,500.

28
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,512
Party guests were quietly reaching for cash

29
00:01:16,512 --> 00:01:17,980
envelopes instead of gold rings.

30
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:19,749
And that was the top.

31
00:01:19,749 --> 00:01:21,086
On the same exchange measure,

32
00:01:21,086 --> 00:01:23,255
one don was 900,000 won at the end of February,

33
00:01:23,255 --> 00:01:24,500
740,000 at the end of June,

34
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,620
and 640,000 by October 26.

35
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:28,411
A few pauses,

36
00:01:28,411 --> 00:01:30,110
but nine months of walking downstairs.

37
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:32,930
So who pushed gold down like this?

38
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:36,170
There are three main reasons.

39
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:38,925
Reason one: interest rates.

40
00:01:38,925 --> 00:01:40,672
On September 16 the U.S.

41
00:01:40,672 --> 00:01:42,783
Federal Reserve raised rates,

42
00:01:42,783 --> 00:01:45,040
its first hike since July 2023.

43
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:46,827
Its answer to oil prices and inflation climbing

44
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:47,840
again on Middle East tensions.

45
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:52,835
The 10-year Treasury yield hit 5.1% on September 23,

46
00:01:52,835 --> 00:01:54,690
the highest since 2007.

47
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:58,780
Here's gold's weakness: it pays no interest at all.

48
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,517
At late-September rates,

49
00:02:00,517 --> 00:02:03,848
10,000 dollars in Treasuries earns about 500 a year.

50
00:02:03,848 --> 00:02:06,410
Gold in a safe is the same a year later.

51
00:02:06,610 --> 00:02:08,536
Holding gold means giving up that interest,

52
00:02:08,536 --> 00:02:10,730
so higher rates make gold more expensive to hold.

53
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:16,740
Reason two: short-term money that piled in during January.

54
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:21,770
Analysts say it amplified the 20%-plus slide as it left.

55
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:24,587
Everyone who crowded into the elevator on the

56
00:02:24,587 --> 00:02:26,680
way up tried to squeeze out at once.

57
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:30,484
Then last week something stranger happened: U.S.

58
00:02:30,484 --> 00:02:32,800
yields fell, and gold fell with them.

59
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,672
One analyst called it the first such joint drop

60
00:02:37,672 --> 00:02:39,660
since December 2008.

61
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:42,817
Many analysts blame last week's drop on the

62
00:02:42,817 --> 00:02:44,297
third reason: raising cash.

63
00:02:44,297 --> 00:02:46,051
When U.S. tech stocks fell hard,

64
00:02:46,051 --> 00:02:49,230
investors who had borrowed to invest suddenly needed cash.

65
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:50,111
At times like that,

66
00:02:50,111 --> 00:02:52,300
gold is often sold first because it turns into cash instantly.

67
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:53,631
Nobody hated gold.

68
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:57,560
It was just the fastest thing in the wallet to turn into cash.

69
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:00,910
Let's sum up the three reasons gold fell.

70
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:04,888
The spring slide came largely from January's crowd leaving,

71
00:03:04,888 --> 00:03:07,186
higher interest weighed in the fall,

72
00:03:07,186 --> 00:03:10,890
and last week's plunge added sellers who needed cash fast.

73
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:13,521
Why did Korean gold fall more?

74
00:03:13,521 --> 00:03:16,573
In short: the exchange rate and the local premium.

75
00:03:16,573 --> 00:03:20,113
Korean gold is the global price times the won-dollar rate,

76
00:03:20,113 --> 00:03:22,310
plus a premium when buyers crowd in.

77
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:23,060
In April,

78
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:25,925
reports said Korean gold traded 7 to 10% above

79
00:03:25,925 --> 00:03:27,669
the global price on average,

80
00:03:27,669 --> 00:03:29,350
and up to 20% on some days.

81
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:31,331
It's hard to split the 8-point gap exactly,

82
00:03:31,331 --> 00:03:33,185
but that premium tends to appear when gold is

83
00:03:33,185 --> 00:03:34,667
hot and shrink when the fever cools.

84
00:03:34,667 --> 00:03:35,697
When the premium shrinks,

85
00:03:35,697 --> 00:03:37,880
Korean gold falls more even at the same global price.

86
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:42,958
So who makes money whether gold rises or falls?

87
00:03:42,958 --> 00:03:46,193
The clue sits where it's busy either way: the

88
00:03:46,193 --> 00:03:48,780
price board at a local jewelry shop.

89
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:50,479
On October 26,

90
00:03:50,479 --> 00:03:53,046
the price board of wholesaler Korea Gold

91
00:03:53,046 --> 00:03:57,090
Exchange showed 750,000 won to buy one don and 640,000 to sell.

92
00:03:57,090 --> 00:04:00,620
Not to be confused with the Korea Exchange gold market.

93
00:04:00,820 --> 00:04:03,920
Same day, same gold, two prices.

94
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:07,710
A 110,000 won gap. Nearly 70,000 of it is VAT,

95
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:10,050
since the buying price includes 10%.

96
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:12,780
The rest goes to the people in the middle.

97
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:17,008
So how much must gold rise just to break even if you buy today?

98
00:04:17,008 --> 00:04:19,950
750,000 divided by 640,000 is 1.17: a 17% rise

99
00:04:19,950 --> 00:04:21,740
just to get your money back.

100
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:25,360
Add a craftsmanship fee for a baby ring and the bar is even higher.

101
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:28,469
Jewelers and wholesalers earn that gap,

102
00:04:28,469 --> 00:04:30,907
minus VAT, every time gold changes hands.

103
00:04:30,907 --> 00:04:32,810
Up or down, the more gold moves,

104
00:04:32,810 --> 00:04:33,940
the more they make.

105
00:04:34,150 --> 00:04:35,714
Last year, as gold rallied,

106
00:04:35,714 --> 00:04:38,320
Korea Gold Exchange's revenue was reported to

107
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:40,231
have doubled to 7.7 trillion won.

108
00:04:40,231 --> 00:04:41,447
Revenue isn't profit,

109
00:04:41,447 --> 00:04:43,590
but the toll road clearly got busier.

110
00:04:43,780 --> 00:04:46,666
Standing on the road gold travels tends to pay

111
00:04:46,666 --> 00:04:49,050
more steadily than guessing its price.

112
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:52,485
Where you buy changes the price structure too.

113
00:04:52,485 --> 00:04:54,203
A jeweler sells physical gold,

114
00:04:54,203 --> 00:04:55,750
with VAT and a wide spread.

115
00:04:55,950 --> 00:04:58,392
The Korea Exchange gold market trades in 1-gram

116
00:04:58,392 --> 00:04:59,898
units in a brokerage account,

117
00:04:59,898 --> 00:05:02,080
with no VAT unless you withdraw the metal.

118
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:07,720
One famous investor openly disliked gold: Warren Buffett.

119
00:05:08,530 --> 00:05:09,826
In February 2012,

120
00:05:09,826 --> 00:05:12,950
Buffett wrote to shareholders about gold.

121
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:15,571
All the world's gold, about 170,000 tons,

122
00:05:15,571 --> 00:05:19,173
melted together would make a cube about 68 feet on each side.

123
00:05:19,173 --> 00:05:21,240
It would fit in a baseball infield.

124
00:05:21,430 --> 00:05:24,200
At the time, it was worth 9.6 trillion dollars.

125
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,093
What else could that money buy? All U.S.

126
00:05:28,093 --> 00:05:30,491
cropland, plus 16 Exxon Mobils,

127
00:05:30,491 --> 00:05:33,120
with a trillion dollars left over.

128
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:35,938
He called that trillion walking-around money.

129
00:05:35,938 --> 00:05:37,857
After a shopping spree like that,

130
00:05:37,857 --> 00:05:39,370
no sense feeling strapped.

131
00:05:40,170 --> 00:05:41,587
Picture a century from now.

132
00:05:41,587 --> 00:05:43,791
The farmland will have grown endless corn,

133
00:05:43,791 --> 00:05:44,684
wheat and cotton,

134
00:05:44,684 --> 00:05:47,780
and the companies will have piled up profits and dividends.

135
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:51,930
The cube? Same size, still producing nothing.

136
00:05:52,730 --> 00:05:54,430
And then he added:

137
00:05:54,630 --> 00:05:56,375
You can fondle the cube,

138
00:05:56,375 --> 00:05:58,120
but it will not respond.

139
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:02,380
Gold did rise afterward: from 1,750 dollars an

140
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:05,540
ounce when he wrote to around 4,000 today.

141
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:08,669
But his point wasn't whether gold goes up.

142
00:06:08,669 --> 00:06:10,762
Gold earns nothing on its own,

143
00:06:10,762 --> 00:06:13,900
so it rises only if someone pays more for it.

144
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:17,352
So Berkshire Hathaway bought what works on its own,

145
00:06:17,352 --> 00:06:19,010
like farmland: businesses.

146
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:23,365
Yet today the steadiest gold buyers are some of

147
00:06:23,365 --> 00:06:25,850
the most conservative institutions on earth.

148
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:27,682
Central banks.

149
00:06:27,682 --> 00:06:30,336
According to the World Gold Council,

150
00:06:30,336 --> 00:06:33,800
they bought 863 tonnes of gold last year alone.

151
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,680
More than 1,000 tonnes a year in each of the three years before.

152
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:39,861
Even as gold falls, the buying continues.

153
00:06:39,861 --> 00:06:42,591
Goldman Sachs estimates central banks bought 91

154
00:06:42,591 --> 00:06:45,030
tonnes a month over the last three months.

155
00:06:45,230 --> 00:06:49,400
Before 2022 it was 17 tonnes a month: more than five times as much.

156
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:51,523
The reason is insurance.

157
00:06:51,523 --> 00:06:54,058
Gold doesn't rely on any government's promise,

158
00:06:54,058 --> 00:06:57,420
so it stays even if something happens to the dollar or bonds.

159
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:00,043
Buffett admitted this too: a century from now,

160
00:07:00,043 --> 00:07:02,730
people will still rush to gold when they're afraid.

161
00:07:02,930 --> 00:07:04,902
Buying gold is buying insurance.

162
00:07:04,902 --> 00:07:07,120
Buying a company is buying a worker.

163
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:09,093
How should we read the gold outlook?

164
00:07:09,093 --> 00:07:09,647
In the short run,

165
00:07:09,647 --> 00:07:11,700
it's more practical to watch the three reasons we just covered.

166
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:15,202
Higher Treasury yields make gold costlier to hold,

167
00:07:15,202 --> 00:07:19,298
a higher won-dollar rate and premium tend to lift Korean gold,

168
00:07:19,298 --> 00:07:22,600
and central-bank buying acts as a supporting hand.

169
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:26,932
But zoom out and the picture changes. In 2000,

170
00:07:26,932 --> 00:07:29,620
gold averaged 279 dollars an ounce.

171
00:07:29,820 --> 00:07:34,193
Today it's around 4,000: roughly 14 times in 26 years.

172
00:07:34,193 --> 00:07:36,542
It fell for years after 2011,

173
00:07:36,542 --> 00:07:38,890
but eventually set new highs.

174
00:07:39,090 --> 00:07:41,819
So many see gold not as something to time,

175
00:07:41,819 --> 00:07:44,222
but as a steady slice of a portfolio.

176
00:07:44,222 --> 00:07:47,211
World Gold Council research puts that slice at

177
00:07:47,211 --> 00:07:49,290
roughly 2 to 9% of total assets.

178
00:07:50,990 --> 00:07:53,399
Back to the ring in the drawer.

179
00:07:53,399 --> 00:07:58,529
The one test I promised: what job does this gold do in your money?

180
00:07:58,529 --> 00:08:00,860
Check it with three questions.

181
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:04,880
One: will you need this money within a year?

182
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:07,515
Then decide by the date you need it,

183
00:08:07,515 --> 00:08:08,540
not the price.

184
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:11,980
No one knows which way gold will move, or when.

185
00:08:12,190 --> 00:08:15,318
Second: do you plan to buy back, or buy fresh?

186
00:08:15,318 --> 00:08:18,650
Then every purchase has to clear that 17% hurdle.

187
00:08:18,850 --> 00:08:21,447
Third: how much of your wealth is gold?

188
00:08:21,447 --> 00:08:23,379
Far above the 2 to 9% we saw,

189
00:08:23,379 --> 00:08:26,575
that's a reason to review it; within that range,

190
00:08:26,575 --> 00:08:28,440
there's less reason to rush.

191
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:30,124
If you do sell,

192
00:08:30,124 --> 00:08:32,069
check the purity mark and weight,

193
00:08:32,069 --> 00:08:34,544
compare offers from two or three jewelers,

194
00:08:34,544 --> 00:08:37,550
and remember the craftsmanship fee won't come back.

195
00:08:37,750 --> 00:08:40,756
And if that ring holds a child's first birthday,

196
00:08:40,756 --> 00:08:43,010
that value isn't on any price board.

197
00:08:43,810 --> 00:08:45,889
One last thing. Gold earns nothing,

198
00:08:45,889 --> 00:08:47,850
so there are no numbers to weigh.

199
00:08:48,050 --> 00:08:49,282
Companies are different.

200
00:08:49,282 --> 00:08:52,722
I jot these numbers down in a notebook every day; that's ValueNote.

201
00:08:52,722 --> 00:08:55,340
I'll leave it in the description if you're curious.

202
00:08:56,140 --> 00:08:58,082
If you want more stories behind the numbers,

203
00:08:58,082 --> 00:08:59,980
please subscribe and turn on notifications.

204
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:02,191
Next notebook: the Coca-Cola stock Warren

205
00:09:02,191 --> 00:09:03,760
Buffett hasn't sold in 38 years.

206
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:04,582
Before that,

207
00:09:04,582 --> 00:09:07,320
watch the story of the Japanese companies he picked.

208
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:09,445
How much gold is in your drawer?

209
00:09:09,445 --> 00:09:11,550
Tell us in the comments. Thank you.
